(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-quiz"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which treatment suits obstructive sleep apnea whose events occur when the patient is supine?","choices":["Positional therapy","Tracheostomy","Palatal implants","Adaptive servo-ventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positional therapy is used when events occur in the supine position: a sleep belt or sleep shirt keeps the patient in a lateral position during sleep, away from the position that triggers the events. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 26"},{"q":"Why does the workup for obesity hypoventilation syndrome include a toxicology screen and thyroid function tests?","choices":["To grade obstructive apnea severity","To confirm awake hypercapnia","To choose between pressure modes","To rule out other causes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation must be excluded. The workup therefore includes a metabolic panel, blood count, thyroid function tests, pulmonary function tests, chest radiograph and toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32, 33"},{"q":"For which patient with obstructive sleep apnea is autotitrating positive airway pressure suited?","choices":["One with central apnea despite pressure therapy","One without significant comorbid conditions","One with significant cardiopulmonary disease","One with neuromuscular weakness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Autotitrating (adjustable) positive airway pressure is used in patients with obstructive sleep apnea who have no significant comorbid conditions. A patient with significant comorbidities falls outside that group. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 12, 26"},{"q":"Which laboratory test belongs in the workup for obstructive sleep apnea when hypothyroidism is suspected?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Serum bicarbonate level","Arterial blood gas analysis","Complete blood count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism is a concomitant medical condition that raises the risk of obstructive sleep apnea, so a thyroid-stimulating hormone level is checked when it is suspected. A polysomnogram still makes the diagnosis of the sleep disorder. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 32"},{"q":"For which people does the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire have some value as a screening tool?","choices":["People already diagnosed with the disorder","Adults at routine yearly wellness visits","Symptomatic people in high-risk settings","Asymptomatic adults in the community"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The tool has some value in highly symptomatic patients in high-risk settings, such as preoperative evaluation. It cannot be used as a true, accurate screening tool in asymptomatic patients, and questionnaires should not routinely screen the community. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How much higher is the prevalence of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease in obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["1.5 times","2 to 3 times","3 to 6 times","10 times"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease has a 2 to 3 times higher prevalence in obstructive sleep apnea. It sits with the other metabolic consequences: insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, raised glucose and triglycerides, and an increased risk of gout. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 20"},{"q":"Which metabolic condition is more prevalent in patients with obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Lower triglyceride levels","Improved insulin sensitivity","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have an increased prevalence of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes mellitus and diabetes complications. The disorder is independently associated with higher glucose and triglycerides and may exacerbate cardiovascular risk in metabolic syndrome. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 15, 20"},{"q":"Which describes hospital care in obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Less use of health care resources","More intensive care and intubation","Fewer hospital admissions","Less long-term care at discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalization rates are higher in obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and patients are more likely to need intensive care, intubation or long-term care at discharge. They also have worse health status and use more health care resources. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the respiratory disturbance index count that the apnea-hypopnea index leaves out?","choices":["Apneas of 10 seconds or more","Hours of total sleep time","Effort-related arousals","Shallow-breathing hypopneas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory disturbance index adds respiratory effort-related arousals: (apneas + hypopneas + arousals) divided by total sleep time in hours. These arousals reflect obstructive upper airway reduction that is not an apnea or a hypopnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which referral is part of patient education for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Neurologist","Sleep physician","Cardiologist","Gastroenterologist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician and proper counseling, alongside risk modification: diet and exercise for weight loss and avoiding alcohol and other sedatives. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 15, 19"},{"q":"How does the prevalence of obstructive sleep apnea change in older adults?","choices":["It increases with age","It decreases with age","It peaks in young adulthood","It does not vary with age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The prevalence of obstructive sleep apnea increases with age, and increasing age is itself a risk factor. In the elderly, mortality from the disorder is lower because patients compensate, yet drowsiness and nocturia persist and improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 11, 17, 22"},{"q":"What drives the unstable breathing pattern of central sleep apnea?","choices":["Decreased sensitivity to carbon dioxide","Upper airway collapse during effort","Decreased intrathoracic pressure","Increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In central sleep apnea the brainstem stops sending signals to breathe: increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide creates an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Airflow stops because effort is lacking, not because the airway collapses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 19, 24"},{"q":"Which finding in an obese patient should prompt screening for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Facial plethora","Low serum bicarbonate","Enlarged tonsils","Morning headaches"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Facial plethora (facial swelling, puffiness) is one of the features that should prompt screening in an obese patient, with unexplained low oxygen saturation, unexplained exertional dyspnea, signs of pulmonary hypertension or right-sided heart failure, and raised serum bicarbonate. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 31, 32"},{"q":"Sleep-disordered breathing is divided into obstructive sleep apnea, central sleep apnea and which third category?","choices":["Periodic limb movement disorder","Mixed apnea with resumed effort","Nighttime reflux disease","Sleep-related hypoventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep-disordered breathing is divided into three categories: obstructive sleep apnea, central sleep apnea and sleep-related hypoventilation. Hypoventilation during sleep reappears in obesity hypoventilation syndrome, where that subgroup is treated with bilevel positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 10, 19, 33"},{"q":"Which patient with suspected obstructive sleep apnea should have a polysomnogram instead of an at-home test?","choices":["One with witnessed apneas","One with suspected moderate disease","One on long-term opioids","One with loud habitual snoring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-term opioid use is one of the conditions that call for a polysomnogram rather than a home kit, along with significant cardiopulmonary disease, neuromuscular weakness, awake hypoventilation or hypoxemia, stroke and severe insomnia. Opioids are also a risk factor for central sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 24"},{"q":"Above what awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is alveolar hypoventilation present for the diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["35 mmHg","40 mmHg","45 mmHg","50 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs awake alveolar hypoventilation, an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mmHg, plus obesity (body mass index above 30) and exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. It is a diagnosis of exclusion. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which pairing of cause and first management step in central sleep apnea is correct?","choices":["High altitude: descend from altitude","Heart failure: begin servo-ventilation","Opioid use: keep opioids, add pressure","High altitude: oral appliance first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea treatment starts with the underlying cause: descend from altitude at high altitude, reduce opioid medications when they are the cause, and optimize heart failure therapy in heart failure. Continuous positive airway pressure is then considered first. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26"},{"q":"Which is the most common form of sleep-disordered breathing?","choices":["Sleep-related hypoventilation","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea is the most common form of sleep-disordered breathing, caused by narrowing of the upper airway from excessive soft tissue or structural abnormalities. Its prevalence increases with age. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 24, 28, 30"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, what neck size scores a point?","choices":["14 inches or larger","15 inches or larger","16 inches or larger","17 inches or larger"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The neck item asks whether the shirt collar is 16 inches (40 cm) or larger, measured around the Adam's apple, and it scores the same for men and women. With 2 or more of the first four questions, a 16-inch neck also places a person at high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 17"},{"q":"For a patient who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure, which surgical option is limited to mild obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Radiofrequency ablation","Tracheostomy","Maxillomandibular advancement","Palatal implants"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Palatal implants are used in patients with mild obstructive sleep apnea who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure. Radiofrequency ablation reaches a little further, to mild to moderate disease in the same intolerant patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What defines obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Absent drive with absent effort","Awake hypoventilation in obesity","Hypoventilation confined to sleep","Airway collapse despite effort"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome, also called Pickwickian syndrome, is awake alveolar hypoventilation (raised awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure) in an obese person that cannot be attributed to other conditions causing alveolar hypoventilation. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24, 28, 30"},{"q":"In which patient are modafinil and armodafinil used with caution?","choices":["One with allergic rhinitis","One with supine-position events","One with hypothyroidism","One with liver disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These stimulants need caution in patients with cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric conditions, so liver disease calls for care. They also have many potential drug interactions, and elderly patients receive lower doses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 11, 12"},{"q":"Which exposure is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Modafinil or armodafinil use","Nasal steroid use","Living at high altitude","Alcohol or sedative use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and avoiding alcohol and other sedatives is part of risk modification, together with diet and exercise for weight loss. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 11, 15, 24"},{"q":"In a split-night polysomnogram, what follows the first 2 hours of recorded sleep when obstructive sleep apnea is significant?","choices":["Continuous positive airway pressure titration","Adaptive servo-ventilation trial","Continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring","A second baseline recording night"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A split-night study records the first 2 hours of sleep to determine severity, then, if obstructive sleep apnea is significant, titrates continuous positive airway pressure: finding the exact air pressure needed to keep the airway open. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 26, 32"},{"q":"Which treatment for sleep-disordered breathing is itself a risk factor for central sleep apnea?","choices":["Continuous positive airway pressure","Positional therapy with a sleep belt","Oral appliance worn during sleep","Nasal expiratory resistance device"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients using continuous positive airway pressure are among those at risk for central sleep apnea. The same therapy is still the first device to consider in central sleep apnea, with adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 24, 26"},{"q":"Which pair of cardiovascular effects accompanies untreated obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Steadier blood pressure, fewer arrhythmias","Lower risk of aortic dissection","More sympathetic tone and arrhythmias","Less sympathetic tone, fewer arrhythmias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea increases sympathetic tone and arrhythmias, and also brings bradycardia, more variable blood pressure and a higher risk of aortic aneurysms and dissections. Arrhythmias and ischemic heart disease are morbidities of untreated disease. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 21"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which set of yes answers places a person at high risk despite a total below 5?","choices":["Body mass index over 35 and male gender","Snoring, tiredness and a 16-inch neck","Snoring, male gender and age over 50","Male gender, age over 50 and 16-inch neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring and tiredness are 2 of the first four questions, and a neck of 16 inches (40 cm) or larger completes a high-risk combination. The total is only 3, yet the person is high risk, as with 2 or more of the first four plus male gender or a body mass index above 35. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which therapy is the most effective treatment for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Oral appliance worn during sleep","Continuous positive airway pressure","Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy with a sleep belt"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Continuous positive airway pressure is the most effective non-pharmacologic therapy for obstructive sleep apnea. Many consequences of untreated disease, such as reflux, raised proinflammatory cytokines, and drowsiness and nocturia in the elderly, improve with it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 19, 22"},{"q":"With 5 or more predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour of sleep, what else confirms obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Body mass index above 30","Serum bicarbonate above 27","A high-risk questionnaire score","A qualifying symptom"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At 5 or more events per hour, one qualifying symptom completes the diagnosis: sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia impairing quality of life; waking with breath holding, gasping or choking; or habitual snoring or breathing interruptions noted by an observer. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 16, 32"},{"q":"For obesity hypoventilation syndrome with sleep-related hypoventilation, which pressure therapy is paired with weight loss?","choices":["Bilevel positive airway pressure","Continuous positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Autotitrating positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When obesity hypoventilation syndrome coexists with sleep-related hypoventilation, first-line therapy pairs a weight loss program with bilevel positive airway pressure. When it coexists with obstructive sleep apnea, continuous positive airway pressure is used instead. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26, 30, 33"}]};
